  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Boletellus chrysenteroides is a striking bolete fungus characterized by its distinctive coloring and texture, belonging to the diverse boletes family found throughout New England. It is an uncommon to rare species that fruiting bodies typically appear in summer and fall months.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Stem reticulation",
      "description": "Features a prominent red or reddish netted pattern (reticulation) on the stem, which is one of the most distinctive characteristics",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore surface color",
      "description": "The underside displays small angular pores that are typically yellow to olive-yellow, sometimes bruising or staining blue-green when handled",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap appearance",
      "description": "Cap is usually brown to grayish-brown with a dry, velvety or finely felted texture",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Boletellus chrysenteroides grows on the forest floor in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests, typically fruiting near decaying wood or in rich soil.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'chrysenteroides' refers to its resemblance to Boletellus chrysenteron, and its blue-green staining reaction when bruised is a common characteristic among many bolete species that helps distinguish them from similar species.",
